  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An area of low pressure could develop off the \nSoutheast coast and bring much needed widespread rainfall to \nsoutheast Georgia and southeast South Carolina this weekend. \n\nFor the rest of the week, the primary weather feature will be \nhigh pressure centered to the north near the Mid Atlantic coast.\nThis setup will drive persistent northeast flow across the \nforecast area and we could see periods of showers develop within\na nearby coastal trough, mainly across the Atlantic coastal \nwaters. These showers could shift onshore at times, mainly \nfurther south along the GA coast. The best chance of this will \ncome in the mid to late week time period. Overall, no \nsignificant rainfall amounts are expected through Thursday. \n\nAs we move into the weekend time period, global forecast models\nseem to be trending towards developing a surface low near the \neast coast of FL and eventually tracking closer to the Southeast\ncoast. There remains considerable uncertainty regarding timing,\nproximity to the coast, and the potential inland extent of any \nrainfall across the forecast area. However, there is gradually \nincreasing potential for a widespread rainfall event. Rainfall \nis much needed for the southeast Georgia and southeast South \nCarolina region as most of the area has received 5% or less than\nnormal rainfall over the last 30 days. Furthermore, CHS \n(-19.74) and SAV (-19.33) are both very close to running a 20 \ninch rainfall deficit year-to-date (through 11/15).",
